Lab 7: Managing Heat (Orchestration Service)
Objectives
Understand OpenStack Heat orchestration service and its components
Create and deploy Heat templates for multi-resource deployments
Scale applications and services using Heat
Monitor and troubleshoot Heat stack events and errors
Prerequisites
OpenStack environment with Heat service installed
OpenStack CLI tools installed (openstack, heat)
Basic understanding of YAML syntax
Valid OpenStack credentials with orchestration permissions
Access to a text editor (Vim, Nano, etc.)
Lab Setup
Environment Verification

# Verify Heat service is available
openstack orchestration service list

# Check available Heat resources
openstack orchestration resource type list
Expected Output: Should display available Heat services and resource types without errors.

Install Heat Client

# For Ubuntu/Debian
sudo apt-get install python3-heatclient

# For CentOS/RHEL
sudo yum install python3-heatclient
Task 1: Create and Deploy Heat Templates
Subtask 1.1: Basic Heat Template Creation
Create a simple Heat template file (basic_stack.yaml):

heat_template_version: 2018-08-31
description: A basic Heat template for lab

resources:
  test_server:
    type: OS::Nova::Server
    properties:
      name: test-server
      image: cirros
      flavor: m1.tiny
Deploy the stack:

openstack stack create -t basic_stack.yaml test-stack
Verify stack creation:

openstack stack list
openstack server list
Troubleshooting Tip: If stack fails, check events with:

openstack stack event list test-stack
Subtask 1.2: Multi-Resource Template
Create an advanced template (multi_resource.yaml):

heat_template_version: 2018-08-31
description: Multi-resource deployment

resources:
  network:
    type: OS::Neutron::Net
    properties:
      name: heat-network
  
  subnet:
    type: OS::Neutron::Subnet
    properties:
      network: { get_resource: network }
      cidr: 192.168.1.0/24
      gateway_ip: 192.168.1.1
  
  server:
    type: OS::Nova::Server
    properties:
      name: multi-resource-server
      image: cirros
      flavor: m1.tiny
      networks:
        - network: { get_resource: network }
Deploy and verify:

openstack stack create -t multi_resource.yaml advanced-stack
openstack stack resource list advanced-stack
Task 2: Scaling Applications with Heat
Subtask 2.1: Auto-Scaling Configuration
Create scaling template (scaling_stack.yaml):

heat_template_version: 2018-08-31
description: Auto-scaling setup

parameters:
  desired_capacity:
    type: number
    default: 2

resources:
  lb_pool:
    type: OS::Neutron::Pool
    properties:
      name: HTTP
      protocol: HTTP
      method: ROUND_ROBIN

  scaled_group:
    type: OS::Heat::AutoScalingGroup
    properties:
      min_size: 1
      max_size: 5
      desired_capacity: { get_param: desired_capacity }
      resource:
        type: OS::Nova::Server
        properties:
          image: cirros
          flavor: m1.tiny
Deploy with scaling:

openstack stack create -t scaling_stack.yaml scaling-stack \
  --parameter desired_capacity=3
Check scaling status:

openstack stack resource show scaling-stack scaled_group
Subtask 2.2: Manual Scaling
Update stack with new capacity:

openstack stack update scaling-stack -t scaling_stack.yaml \
  --parameter desired_capacity=4
Verify new instances:

openstack server list
Task 3: Troubleshooting Heat Stacks
Subtask 3.1: Stack Event Analysis
Check stack events:

openstack stack event list scaling-stack
View detailed error for failed resource:

openstack stack event show scaling-stack <resource_name> <event_id>
Subtask 3.2: Stack Debugging
Get stack template:

openstack stack template show scaling-stack
Check resource health:

openstack stack resource list scaling-stack --nested-depth 2
Common troubleshooting commands:

# View stack outputs
openstack stack output list scaling-stack

# Check Heat engine logs (on controller node)
sudo tail -f /var/log/heat/heat-engine.log
